    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d325">

                <pb facs="0535" n="249" xml:id="pg249"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/2636</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spring
                        Rice</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 111</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 25, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 27</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to your note of the 22d
                    instant I have the honour to inform you that according to a telegram which I
                    have received from Sir Edward Grey the
                    instructions of October 20 for the guidance of British sea captains, which have
                    been communicated to your Government contain the orders at present in force.</p>
                <p>You will no doubt observe that the instructions contained in your note under
                    reply were issued before the above-named date.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cecil Spring Rice</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
